WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.906
Can a language model choose a more sensible next token by
considering related candidates together?

00:00:05.906 --> 00:00:10.197
This matters to researchers and developers seeking better text
generation.

00:00:10.197 --> 00:00:14.017
Greedy decoding simply picks the single most probable token.

00:00:14.017 --> 00:00:19.311
After “I put ketchup on my,” we normally expect food, yet greedy
decoding selects face.

00:00:19.311 --> 00:00:23.660
Salad, burger, and sandwich rank highly, with salad only
slightly below face.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:28.750
Their combined probability is over twice face's, consistent with
that expectation.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:35.193
My rule groups supported candidates by meaning and applies two
thresholds, keeping the model's probabilities unchanged.

00:00:35.193 --> 00:00:38.558
Changing a parameter reruns selection, not GPT-2.

00:00:38.558 --> 00:00:44.588
At default settings, face changes to salad in the ketchup case;
the other four remain unchanged.

00:00:44.588 --> 00:00:52.900
These five related examples give me initial confidence in the
approach, but its benefits on new prompts still need testing.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:59.400
Next, I would expand semantic categories and test more varied
inputs.
